   },
   "order": 22,
   "mode": 0,
   "inputs": [],
   "outputs": [],
   "properties": {
    "text": "VISUAL LOOK PRESETS — ASK CODEX\n\nName a look in your workflow request. Example: Use 28, Blacklight Botanical Takeover. Add any camera direction to the shots that need it.\n\nThe preset gives the sequence its visual treatment. The shot plan controls what happens and what the camera sees. This note is a catalog. Ask Codex to revise the workflow to change the look.\n\n01. Home Video VHS\nMuted tape colors, color bleed and tracking noise.\n\n02. Symmetrical Storybook Cinema\nPastel color, soft light and theatrical design.\n\n03. CCTV / Security Camera\nDesaturated, compressed surveillance texture.\n\n04. Living Polaroid\nFaded instant-film color and chemical mottling.\n\n05. Super 8 Vacation Film\nWarm film grain, flicker and gate weave.\n\n06. Direct-Flash Disposable Camera\nHard frontal light and sharp shadows.\n\n07. Midnight Infrared\nGray or green night vision.\n\n08. Miniature Stop-Motion World\nHandmade materials and miniature scale.\n\n09. 1990s Local News\nFlat broadcast light and standard-definition texture.\n\n10. Early Camera Phone\nDigital noise and blocky compression.\n\n11. 1970s Grindhouse\nLurid color and worn theatrical print.\n\n12. Bleach-Bypass Thriller\nSteel tones, dense blacks and silver highlights.\n\n13. Dreamy Optical Diffusion\nPastel haze and blooming highlights.\n\n14. Silent-Era Film\nMonochrome archival film and print wear.\n\n15. Underwater Housing\nSubmerged color, particles and caustics.\n\n16. Thermal Camera\nFalse-color or white-hot thermal imagery.\n\n17. Velvet Nightmare\nCrimson velvet, warm pools of light, green shadows, and quiet theatrical unease.\n\n18. Fluorescent Institutional Horror\nAging green-white fluorescent light and faded institutional colors.\n\n19. Haunted Photochemical Emulsion\nBruised colors and film emulsion that seems to decay on screen.\n\n20. Forensic Flash Nightmare\nCold frontal illumination and invasive evidence-photo detail.\n\n21. Domestic Uncanny\nPastel domestic warmth with oppressive order.\n\n22. Impossible Moonlight\nPale silver-blue light and crisp shadows with subtly impossible geometry.\n\n23. Anatomical Waxwork\nGlasslike eyes and translucent wax surfaces on recognizable characters.\n\n24. Drowned Gothic\nWaterlogged materials, slate light, and weak amber practicals.\n\n25. Expressionist Shadow Theater\nAngular sets and graphic, diagonal shadows.\n\n26. Temporal Afterimage Horror\nA photographic image with faint traces of earlier motion.\n\n27. Primary Color Gravity Riot\nHard primary colors and architecture with conflicting gravity.\n\n28. Blacklight Botanical Takeover\nOversized ultraviolet plants reclaim a recognizable room.\n\n29. The Room Is a Collage\nTorn photographs and painted cutouts become physical architecture.\n\n30. The Rainbow Has Weight\nSolid slabs of saturated colored light refract the room.\n\n31. Cardboard Cosmos\nFull-scale theatrical planets and visibly handmade scenery.\n\n32. Carnival of Wrong Scale\nEveryday objects take on impossible architectural proportions.\n\n33. The Chromatic Eclipse\nA dark eclipse motivates coral rim light and ultramarine fill.\n\n34. Walking Through the Wrong Shadow\nCast shadows contain rooms with real depth.\n\n35. The Room Unfolds (Pop-Up Modular Interior)\nA room built from oversized, hinged pop-up panels.\n\n36. Midnight on a Mirrored Planet\nBlack mirror terrain, pink salt formations, and a green sky.\n\n37. Living Anatomy — Organic Interior\nArchitecture made of skin, teeth, muscle, hair, and bone.\n\n38. Hidden Worlds — Cinematic Nature Documentary\nLifelike natural history with accurate macro optics and anatomy.\n\n39. Animated Feature — Expressive 3D Storytelling\nPixar-inspired dimensional animation with expressive character poses.\n\n40. The New Flesh — Clinical Body Horror\nCronenberg-inspired practical prosthetics and clinical lighting.",
